<!-- EduBotics in-repo copy of the Edu:1 (5dof_assembly_urdf2) SolidWorks export.
     Loaded by the React 3D twin (components/UrdfTwin.jsx, URDF_ASSETS.edu1) and
     parsed by the INDEPENDENT FK oracle in
     physical_ai_server/test/test_edu1_ik.py, so a transcription error in
     workflow/edu1_ik.py's baked constants cannot verify itself.

     FOUR deliberate SEMANTIC differences from the raw CAD export. There are
     three further differences that are not semantic and are listed after them,
     because "and nothing else" was wrong and the line-ending one in particular
     defeats the clean-diff hope this header ends on:

     1. robot name "5dof_assembly_urdf2" -> "edu1_studio" (the CAD name starts
        with a digit and means nothing outside SolidWorks; nothing reads it).
     2. mesh filenames "package://5dof_assembly_urdf2/meshes/X" -> "./meshes/X",
        so the browser fetches them relative to this file with no ROS resolver
        and no CDN (classroom offline requirement) — same shape as
        public/edu6-urdf/edu6.urdf.
     3. Every joint LIMIT that is a right angle is written to the same
        precision the rest of this file already uses: 1.57 -> 1.5708 and
        3.14 -> 3.1416. The exporter wrote the ORIGINS at four decimals
        ("rpy=-1.5708 0 1.5708") and the LIMITS at two, which is a rounding of
        the designer's 90 deg / 180 deg, not a different intent — the edu6
        export writes both at four. It is not cosmetic: joint5 is the jaw-roll
        axis and its window IS the jaw-fold window, so at +-1.57 a required
        roll of exactly +-90 deg (a cube sitting square-on to the arm's own
        bearing, not a corner case) fell 0.0008 rad OUTSIDE both folded
        twins and the grasp was refused with „Arbeitsbereich". 0.0008 rad is
        half a servo tick, so nothing physical changes; only the arithmetic
        stops rejecting its own design intent.
     4. RL_joint and its LF_joint <mimic> have their axes FLIPPED, turning the
        claw band from 0 -> -1.57 into 0 -> +1.57. Zero is the same physical
        pose (jaws closed) either way; this makes OPEN the numerically LARGER
        value, which every shared code path assumes:
          * motion.check_grasp_held reads HELD as an achieved angle ABOVE the
            commanded close,
          * object_catalog validates gripper_close_rad inside a [closed, open)
            band,
          * SimArm classifies a command BELOW its close threshold as a close.
        The driver carries the same flip through its per-joint sign vector, so
        /joint_states, this twin and the servo agree. Kept as a URDF edit rather
        than a rename so a future CAD re-export still diffs cleanly against it.

     NON-SEMANTIC differences, i.e. the rest of the answer to "what else":

     5. LINE ENDINGS. The raw export is CRLF (509 of them, pure ASCII); this
        copy is LF. That is why an un-normalised `diff` against the export
        reports 1065 changed lines (every line of both files) where the real
        figure is 113. A future CAD re-export will therefore NOT "diff cleanly"
        against this file unless it is normalised first — do that.
     6. This header comment.
     7. Two inline "AXIS FLIPPED" comments inside RL_joint and LF_joint.
     Consequence of 6: this copy carries 12 non-ASCII bytes (German quotes and
     dashes) where the export is pure ASCII. Neither file has a BOM and neither
     ends with a newline.

     Verified: 184 elements each, identical tag sequence, identical element
     text, exactly 28 elements with differing attributes — 1 robot name,
     18 mesh filenames, and 9 accounted for by differences 3 and 4.

     The meshes beside this file are vertex-clustering DECIMATIONS of the CAD
     STLs (15.007 MB -> 3.420 MB, 4.39x). Deviation on the worst mesh
     (base_link, worst on every metric), re-measured 2026-09-05 with exact
     point-to-triangle distances — SAY WHICH METRIC, because the three differ
     by 3x and this header used to quote a fourth number (0.58 mm) that
     reproduces under none of nine variants tried:
       * decimated vertex -> nearest RAW VERTEX          0.479 mm
       * decimated vertex -> nearest raw TRIANGLE        0.473 mm
       * raw vertex -> nearest decimated TRIANGLE        0.675 mm  <- the
         symmetric point-to-surface figure, i.e. the honest worst case
       * raw vertex -> nearest DECIMATED VERTEX          1.579 mm  (a
         cluster-representative distance; NOT a surface error)
     Bounding boxes are preserved to 0.0133 mm and every deviation is INWARD
     (link2 z-max, link3 z-min, right_finger z-min/z-max; the other 6 meshes
     are bit-identical), so a box derived from these meshes is never smaller
     than the raw geometry. They are a VISUAL asset — the collision model is
     workflow/arm_geometry.py::EDU1_LINK_BOXES, which is derived from the
     full-resolution meshes and rounded OUTWARD by 0.1 mm; that rounding
     absorbs the 0.0133 mm with >= 0.089 mm to spare. -->
